    <%# == The unannotated tests THEMSELVES, for the place the reader picked
        The last rung of the ladder the Overview panel opens and "How SpecGuard reads this suite, by
        area" narrows. The Overview used to print "Not visible to SpecGuard" as `total_specs_count -
        annotated_specs_count` and say *"SpecGuard cannot see the other N tests"*; the ranking above
        names the AREAS that count is concentrated in. Neither names a TEST, and until this panel the
        only route to one was `GET /api/v1/repository?unannotated_examples=1&spec_directory=…` with
        an API key — a route off the product, for the reader the product had just handed a worklist
        to.

        SPGD-711 then corrected what may be said ABOUT these rows without changing which rows they
        are. Every example here lacks an authored `@intent` — exact, and the same predicate the
        Overview subtracts. Most of them SpecGuard nonetheless READS, from the description the client
        sends and this platform stores; the last column shows exactly what it read, so a reader can
        check the reading rather than take a badge's word for it. The rows it read nothing from come
        FIRST, because the list is capped at a hundred and those are the ones that must not fall off
        the page.

        Rendered only when a narrowing was ASKED for, and the read is taken only then. The model's
        narrowings are OPTIONAL and whole-run is a complete ask on the JSON endpoint; this surface
        deliberately does not take it, for the reason the controller states: a hundred rows of debt
        nobody asked for is the Overview's subtraction again at length.

        NO NEW PARAMETER. It rides `?spec_directory=` and `?spec_file=`, so one ask opens EVERY
        panel that reads it, each answering in its own grain over the same area. `show.html.erb`
        states the rule at "Areas that grew or shrank": that is how `drill_down_path` composes asks,
        and it is not a collision to be fixed by minting a second parameter.

        NO "Close" BUTTON, and its absence is a decision rather than an omission. The three panels
        that carry one each OWN an ask, and closing it is the gesture that dismisses them. This panel
        owns neither of the asks it rides: a "Close" here would have to clear `?spec_directory=`,
        which would close the two panels that ask belongs to as a side effect — the precise inversion
        of the carry-through rule `RepositoriesHelper#drill_down_path` exists to keep ("closing an
        area is not a request to close the file", and in every other direction). The reader dismisses
        this panel with the Close button of the panel that owns the ask, which is where they set it.

        It PRESENTS and does not judge — the rule `UnannotatedExamples` and `UnannotatedDirectories`
        both state, and it governs this partial too: rows, and never a fraction, a threshold or a
        colour-coded verdict on one of them.

        Same visibility rule and same gate as the panels around it: read-only suite telemetry on
        `current_repository(:view)`, outside `manage_keys`, and nothing here widens that. %>
    <% if @unannotated_examples %>
      <%# What this population was narrowed BY, as the object holds it — read off the panel's own
          subject rather than off the request ivars, the discipline the area→file link one panel over
          already keeps. Both may be present, in which case the read AND-ed them (see
          `SpecObservation.unannotated_in`, where the absence of a precedence rule is argued), and
          every sentence below has to describe the intersection rather than either half. %>
      <% narrowings = [@unannotated_examples.spec_file, @unannotated_examples.spec_directory].compact %>
      <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Unannotated tests here", id: "unannotated-examples",
                                        class: "lg:col-span-3") do %>
        <% if narrowings.size > 1 %>
          <%# WHAT THE READ NARROWED BY, hoisted OUT of the caption below and above the branch,
              because it is a disclosure about the POPULATION rather than a claim about the list —
              SPGD-363's rule, and this is the branch that proves it. Two asks are AND-ed and neither
              wins (`SpecObservation.unannotated_in` refuses a precedence rule), so a file outside
              that area returns NOTHING — and the state a reader then meets is the empty one, where a
              caption living inside the rows-present branch has already vanished. Told only that
              there is "nothing unannotated" in two paths that each hold plenty, they would read a
              rendering bug; the honest answer is that the intersection is empty. %>
          <p class="text-xs text-app-muted mb-3">
            Both asks narrow this list and a row must match each of them, so a file outside that area
            is an empty intersection rather than one of the two having been dropped.
          </p>
        <% end %>
        <% if @unannotated_examples.rows.any? %>
          <%# THE CAP, folded in the view rather than asked of the object. `UnannotatedExamples`
              refuses a `truncated?` and says why: a predicate no surface calls is a claim nothing has
              ever checked, and it may only arrive WITH the spec that runs it against an empty and a
              capped read. Its class comment names the alternative in as many words — *"`recorded_count
              > rows.size` is a client's sentence to write"* — and this is the client. Both operands
              are the object's own integers off one read, and this asks only which is larger; the same
              shape "How SpecGuard reads this suite, by area" folds `carries_debt` in, one grain up. %>
          <% truncated = @unannotated_examples.recorded_count > @unannotated_examples.rows.size %>

          <%# What the list IS, before the list — the discipline every panel on this page keeps. Every
              figure is counted off THE SAME READ that returned the rows: the population count rides
              back as a window (`SpecObservation::UNANNOTATED_POPULATION_COUNTS`) and the window is
              evaluated after the WHERE, so it describes the NARROWED population rather than the run,
              and a caption cannot come to describe a different row set from the table under it. %>
          <p class="text-xs text-app-muted mb-3" id="unannotated-examples-basis">
            <%# The subject, named before anything is claimed about it. An empty state without a
                subject is a sentence about nothing, and so is a caption — "all 3 examples this run
                recorded here" is not a fact until "here" has been said. %>
            <% if @unannotated_examples.spec_file %>
              <span class="font-mono text-app-content-secondary"><%= @unannotated_examples.spec_file %></span>
            <% end %>
            <% if narrowings.size > 1 %>and<% end %>
            <% if @unannotated_examples.spec_directory %>
              <span class="font-mono text-app-content-secondary"><%= @unannotated_examples.spec_directory %></span>
            <% end %>
            —
            <%# The cap, disclosed whether or not anything was cut, for the reason every capped list
                here states: `rows.size` cannot state the population — it IS the truncated figure — so
                a list whose length happens to equal a limit the reader cannot see reads as the whole
                story. The two sentences are written out rather than assembled, because the untruncated
                one is a claim about the whole of what was recorded and the truncated one is a claim
                about a head, and a shared clause would let an edit meant for one land on both.

                "In the order you would open them" and never "worst first": this read RANKS NOTHING —
                `SpecObservation.unannotated_in` orders by `spec_file_path`, then `line_number`, then
                `id`, and says in full that it is a worklist rather than a ranking. A caption promising
                an order nothing measured is the defect `#spec_file_examples_scope_sentence` is five
                sentences long to avoid. %>
            <% if truncated %>
              The first <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.rows.size) %> of the
              <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.recorded_count) %> examples this run
              recorded here without an <code>@intent</code>, in the order you would open them — by
              spec file, then by line.
              <%# The population the read DROPPED, which is the one disclosure that would survive
                  every branch of this caption if the other branches could reach it (they cannot: an
                  uncut list dropped nothing, and an empty read has nothing to drop). Stated as what
                  is missing rather than as a bare limit, because a reader who annotates these and
                  re-delivers is exactly the reader the remainder is a fact about. %>
              The other
              <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.recorded_count - @unannotated_examples.rows.size) %>
              are not on this page: this is a worklist capped at
              <%= number_with_delimiter(SpecObservation::UNANNOTATED_EXAMPLES_LIMIT) %>, so it is the
              head of what is unannotated here and not the whole of it.
            <% else %>
              All <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.recorded_count) %>
              <%= "example".pluralize(@unannotated_examples.recorded_count) %> this run recorded here
              without an <code>@intent</code>, in the order you would open them — by spec file, then
              by line.
            <% end %>
            <%# The two paths, said once, where a reader meets the column that shows them — the same
                sentence "Examples in this spec file" carries, and needed more here: that panel is
                keyed on one including file, and this list spans files and is ORDERED by the one that
                RAN each example, so the column a reader sorts by and the coordinate they go and open
                are two different files on exactly the rows a shared example group produced. %>
            Each row names the spec file that RAN the example, and under the name where it is
            DEFINED — the same file for every ordinary example, and a different one for an example a
            shared example group defines elsewhere.
            <%# Named, not scored. `UnannotatedDirectories` refuses a fraction one grain up and says
                why; at THIS grain the temptation is a verdict on the row rather than a percentage
                over it, and the refusal is the same one. %>
            Named and not scored: nothing here says whether a test ought to carry an
            <code>@intent</code>, only that this run recorded it without one.
            <%# THE SPLIT SPGD-711 PUT IN THIS CAPTION. Every row here is unannotated, which is
                exact — and this list used to be captioned, on the API and the MCP bridge, as the
                tests SpecGuard cannot see, which was not. Both figures come off windows over the
                same rows the table shows (see `UnannotatedExamples#derived_count`), so the sentence
                cannot describe a different slice from the list, and the unreadable rows are at the
                HEAD of the list by the read's own ordering — so on a cut page they are the ones a
                reader is looking at rather than the ones past the cap. %>
            <%# THREE BRANCHES AND NOT A SENTENCE PLUS A QUALIFIER, on the Overview's precedent
                two panels up. `show.html.erb` branches the same claim on `derived.positive?` and
                says why: where nothing was derived, "SpecGuard reads N of them from the test's own
                description" describes an EMPTY SET, "shown in the last column" points at a column
                showing the opposite, and the caveat after it warns a reader about the weakness of a
                reading NOTHING on the page rests on — while "the other M" and "those are listed
                first" are both said of a population that is the whole list. That branch is not
                exotic here: narrow to `spec/requests` and the ordinary `POST /api/v1/ingest rejects
                a malformed body` shape lands in it, which is `DerivedIntent`'s own canonical
                unreadable example. Each branch is written out whole rather than assembled from a
                shared clause, on this caption's existing rule — see the truncated/untruncated
                pair above, where a shared clause would let an edit meant for one land on both. %>
            <% if @unannotated_examples.derived_count.zero? %>
              SpecGuard reads none of them from the test's own description either — the last column
              says so row by row. These are the tests it genuinely cannot see: not merely tests
              without an <code>@intent</code>, but tests whose description does not give it an
              entity, an action and a behavior.
            <% elsif @unannotated_examples.unreadable_count.positive? %>
              SpecGuard reads <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %>
              of them from the test's own description — shown in the last column, and a weaker
              reading than an <code>@intent</code>: no preconditions, and a layer inferred from the
              directory rather than declared.
              The other <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.unreadable_count) %>
              it cannot read at all, and those are listed first.
            <% else %>
              SpecGuard reads all <%= number_with_delimiter(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %>
              <%= "example".pluralize(@unannotated_examples.derived_count) %> here from the test's
              own description — shown in the last column, and a weaker reading than an
              <code>@intent</code>: no preconditions, and a layer inferred from the directory rather
              than declared.
              There is none here it cannot read at all.
            <% end %>
            <%# Counted off THIS RUN's per-example rows. The Overview's figure is a subtraction over
                the run's own counters, and while both sides read the same `status` the payload wrote,
                the two populations are not guaranteed identical — and this list is narrowed on top of
                that. So the figures here are not promised to add up to the headline, and saying so is
                cheaper than a reader discovering it with a calculator. %>
            Counted off the per-example rows this run reported, which is a different population from
            the suite size on the Overview panel above and may legitimately differ from it.
          </p>

          <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Test", "Spec file", "What SpecGuard reads"],
                                            describedby: "unannotated-examples-basis") do %>
            <% @unannotated_examples.rows.each do |observation| %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content">
                  <%# Through the same two seams every per-example list on this page renders a row
                      through, so one example cannot be named one way in a ranking and another way in
                      the worklist: `#label` falls back to the location for a producer that sent no
                      name, and the location line below is rendered only when there IS a name, so a
                      row wearing the fallback does not wear it twice.

                      `#location_label` is `file_path` + `line_number` — the DEFINITION site — and
                      specifically NOT `spec_file_path` + `line_number`, which for a shared example
                      group pairs two halves from different files and points at whatever happens to
                      sit on that line of the including one. On this panel that would send a reader
                      to annotate a test that is not there.

                      THE COORDINATE IS A LINK, and it is a link on BOTH branches. The reader's next
                      action here is singular and known — open that file at that line and write an
                      `@intent` — and this is the one panel on the page whose whole purpose is to
                      hand them that action. A row from a producer that sent no name WEARS the
                      coordinate as its label, so linking only the location line below would leave
                      inert exactly the rows `#label` exists for: the ones a reader "can neither
                      identify nor go and find".

                      PINNED TO `@latest_test_run.commit_sha` AND NOT TO `main`. That is the run this
                      panel's rows were read off — the reader's anchor, so a page opened on an older
                      run via `?commit_sha=` links into THAT run's tree. `file_path`/`line_number`
                      are a last known path rather than an identity (SPGD-114): a coordinate is
                      accurate against the tree it was recorded from and no other, and linking at
                      `main` would send the reader to whatever has since drifted onto line 412.
                      `commit_sha` is `null: false`, and this partial renders only where
                      `@latest_test_run` exists, so there is no nil branch and no fallback ref.

                      Built off `file_path` and never `spec_file_path` — the same constraint
                      `#location_label` is written to above, inherited exactly rather than restated:
                      the link is that method's two halves, not the row's sort column. The "Spec
                      file" cell beside this one keeps rendering `spec_file_path` and is not a link.

                      A run whose sha was never pushed produces a link GitHub answers 404 to. That is
                      GitHub telling the truth about an unpushed commit, on the same sha this page
                      already prints in "Recent runs" — and this panel PRESENTS and does not judge,
                      so there is no guard, no probe and no conditional rendering.

                      NEW TAB, introduced here deliberately — the app has had no external-link
                      convention because until now it had no link that leaves it. This one does, and
                      the reader is mid-worklist: annotating a test and losing a hundred-row list
                      narrowed by two asks would make the panel worse to use the more of it you work
                      through. `rel` is written out rather than left to the browsers that imply it.

                      ZERO NEW QUERIES: `@repository` and `@latest_test_run` are both already loaded
                      by the time this partial renders, and `#github_blob_url` is string composition
                      that asks GitHub nothing. %>
                  <% definition_site_url = @repository.github_blob_url(observation.file_path,
                                                                       observation.line_number,
                                                                       @latest_test_run.commit_sha) %>
                  <% if observation.name.present? %>
                    <%= observation.label %>
                    <span class="block font-mono text-xs text-app-muted">
                      <%= link_to observation.location_label, definition_site_url,
                                  class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                                         "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content",
                                  target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %>
                    </span>
                  <% else %>
                    <%= link_to observation.label, definition_site_url,
                                class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                                       "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content",
                                target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %>
                  <% end %>
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content-secondary">
                  <%# The file that RAN it, which is the column this list is ordered by — shown
                      because a worklist whose sort key is invisible reads as unordered, and because
                      it is the file the reader opens to reach the example even when the definition
                      site above sits somewhere else. %>
                  <%= observation.spec_file_path %>
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-xs text-app-content-secondary">
                  <%# WHAT SPECGUARD GOT FROM THE DESCRIPTION, or a plain statement that it got
                      nothing — the column that stops this list being captioned as tests the product
                      cannot see.

                      The three fields are shown rather than a badge reading "derived", because the
                      reader's question is not "did a regex match" but "is what SpecGuard read
                      actually right about my test". Shown, they can be checked; summarised, they
                      have to be trusted. `layer` is deliberately absent from the cell: it is the
                      guessed field (`DerivedIntent::DEFAULT_LAYER`) and the caption already says the
                      layer is inferred, so printing it per row would spend the most space on the
                      least evidence.

                      No new query. `SpecObservation#derived_intent` runs the same rule the read
                      counted with, in Ruby, over columns this row already carries. %>
                  <% derived = observation.derived_intent %>
                  <% if derived %>
                    <span class="font-mono text-app-content"><%= derived.entity %></span>
                    <span class="font-mono text-app-content"><%= derived.action %></span>
                    <span class="block text-app-muted"><%= derived.behavior %></span>
                  <% else %>
                    <span class="text-app-muted">Nothing — no <code>@intent</code>, and the
                      description does not give an entity, an action and a behavior.</span>
                  <% end %>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        <% elsif @unannotated_directories&.recorded? %>
          <%# NOTHING CAME BACK FROM A RUN THAT DID WRITE PER-EXAMPLE ROWS — the good news, and the
              state the whole metric exists to reach. `UnannotatedExamples` says so: a fully-annotated
              read is the SUCCESS case rather than the exotic one, and the surface says "nothing left"
              rather than erroring on it.

              Decided off `@unannotated_directories#recorded?` and NOT off a second query. That object
              is already on this page whenever there is a run, its `recorded?` answers exactly "did
              this run write per-example rows at all", and it was counted off a read the page has
              already paid for. `UnannotatedExamples` deliberately cannot answer it — its own comment
              points a client at the sibling block for the same reason — so asking it would mean either
              a predicate on that object with no way to tell the two states apart, or a query bought
              to learn something the page already knows.

              WHAT THIS STATE CANNOT SEPARATE, said out loud rather than left for the reader to
              discover: a place whose every example carries an intent and a place this run recorded
              NOTHING in produce the same empty list. The model refuses to tell them apart here and
              says where a client can. Claiming only the first would be *Vacuous Green* at the grain of
              an empty state — "nothing to check" wearing the spelling of "everything passed" — so the
              description states the finding AND the other reading, and points at the panel that
              settles it. That panel is on this page by construction: `?spec_directory=` renders "Spec
              files in this directory" and `?spec_file=` renders "Examples in this spec file", under
              the same guard this one is rendered under. %>
          <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                title: "Nothing unannotated here",
                description: "This run recorded no example without an @intent in " \
                             "#{narrowings.to_sentence}. Every example it did record there carries " \
                             "one — and so does a place it recorded nothing in at all, since a " \
                             "population of no examples has no unannotated ones either, and this " \
                             "list cannot tell those two apart. The " \
                             "#{@unannotated_examples.spec_directory ? "“Spec files in this directory”" : "“Examples in this spec file”"} " \
                             "panel, open on the same ask, names what this run actually recorded there."
              ) %>
        <% else %>
          <%# A run with no per-example rows AT ALL — ingested before those rows existed, or from a
              client that sends only totals. The same pair "How SpecGuard reads this suite, by area" keeps
              apart one grain up, and the one thing this state may not do is read as the state above
              it: "nobody sent the detail" and "nothing is unannotated" are the same empty table and
              opposite news.

              The panel stays rather than disappearing, because the reader asked for this list and an
              absent panel is a third indistinguishable thing on a page where the Overview may be
              printing a five-figure invisible count at the same time. %>
          <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                title: "No per-example detail on this run",
                description: "This run reported suite totals without the per-example rows SpecGuard " \
                             "names tests from, so there is nothing to list for " \
                             "#{narrowings.to_sentence} — not that everything there is annotated. " \
                             "Any count on the Overview above is taken from the run's own totals."
              ) %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
